// supply JavaScript (safety model: no arbitrary code). All user-derived
// values (base URL, endpoint paths) are JSON-escaped by the renderer before
// they touch this template.
//
// WHY ramping-arrival-rate (open model): k6 schedules iterations at a TARGET
// RATE regardless of how long each request takes. A closed model ("N virtual
// users looping") slows down when the server slows down, which HIDES the
// collapse we are trying to measure (coordinated omission). The open model
// keeps applying the planned arrival rate and lets the queue grow — which is
// exactly what "hitting the wall" looks like.
//
// WHY thresholds with abortOnFail: this is safety layer 3 (Pydantic → runner
// re-check → THIS). If layers 1-2 are somehow bypassed, k6 itself aborts when
// the error rate crosses the safety limit or p95 crosses 5s. delayAbortEval
// gives a few seconds of grace so one noisy bucket doesn't kill the run —
// mirrors SafetyLimits.consecutive_seconds semantics.
// =============================================================================

